# THE SIFREI ROWS on Numbers 26 (THE NUMBERS WALK sitting 8 — THE SECOND CENSUS, 2026-09-11): piska 132 by POSITION — the one piska
# between 131 (25:1) and 133 (27:1); FOUR rows at the shelf's row grain on 26:53-56, each opened and verdicted; the middah named where the
# row argues by one (logic/MIDDOT.md). The Sifrei has NO ROW on 26:1-52 or 26:57-65 (asserted in census_ink.py — the roster read on
# Onkelos alone). Row 3's English HEAD is mistyped "26:25" for 26:55 (the sixth mistyped head of the walk — placed by its quotation, the
# Hebrew row opening with 26:55's first word "only"); row 1's "(Ibid. 59)" is 26:54's clause and row 3's "(Judges 15:13)" is Joshua
# 15:13 — two mistyped citations inside the rows, read to their verses (RESEARCH_LOG.md). The piska was QUICK-LOOKED at THE TENT's
# daughters ("outside the span, not counted") — read whole here (credit guard 1).
from census_ink import *
S = {}
def srow(p, r, verdict, prose): S[(p, r)] = (verdict, prose)

srow(132, 1, 'MATERIAL', '"TO THESE SHALL THE LAND BE APPORTIONED" — THE INCLUSION READ WIDE, THEN FOUR EXCLUSIONS BY FOUR VERSES; THE FOUR-WAY DISPUTE ON WHO THE LAND WAS DIVIDED TO; "THE DEAD INHERIT THE LIVING". "To these shall the land be apportioned as an inheritance according to the number of names" (26:53): "I would understand that ALL are included — Israelites, priests, Levites, proselytes, women, bondsmen, those of indeterminate sex and hermaphrodites"; then the EXCLUSIONS, each by a verse: "in their land you shall not inherit" (18:20) — the PRIESTS out; "in the midst of the children of Israel they shall not inherit" (18:24) — the LEVITES out; "by the names of the tribes of their fathers shall they inherit" (26:55) — PROSELYTES AND BONDSMEN out; "to a man according to his numbers shall his inheritance be given" (the export cites "Ibid. 59" — the clause is 26:54\'s; a mistyped citation read to its verse) — WOMEN, the indeterminate and the hermaphrodite out (the priests\' and Levites\' exclusion is THE INK\'S OWN reason at 26:62, "for no inheritance was given them among the children of Israel" — computed; the women\'s exclusion is the premise the daughters\' plea of chapter 27 argues against, and 26:33 is the roster\'s row for it); THE DISPUTE: R. YOSHIYAH — the land was apportioned to THOSE WHO LEFT EGYPT ("by the names of the tribes of their fathers"), and "to these" excludes MINORS (under twenty); R. YONATAN — to THOSE WHO ENTERED THE LAND ("to these"), and "by the names of their fathers" teaches that God CHANGED THIS INHERITANCE from every inheritance in the Torah: everywhere the living inherit the dead, here THE DEAD INHERIT THE LIVING; REBBI\'s parable — two brothers, priests, one with one son and one with three, go to the granary, take one and three measures, then bring them to their fathers\' father and divide equally (the portions pass UP to the dead fathers and are re-divided); R. SHIMON B. ELAZAR — to THESE AND TO THESE: a man of the exodus took with the exodus, a man of the entry with the entry, a man of both with both, "so that both verses are satisfied" — a PARAMETER ROW with three settings (land_divided_among = left_egypt / entered / both), the exam\'s Bava Batra 117a (already in the inheritance docket at THE TENT, credited there: this is the row\'s READING). The middah: E10-style inclusion-then-exclusion by verses (ribui u-miut — the inclusion and the limitation), and the "two verses satisfied" resolution (I13 — two verses that contradict until a third decides, here decided by the union). The exam: Bava Batra 117a-b (the three views and the parable), 118b (Joshua and Caleb\'s portions), Mishnah Bava Batra 8:1-2 (the order of inheritance), Sifrei Zuta on 26:53 outside scope.')
srow(132, 2, 'MATERIAL', '"TO THE MANY INCREASE, TO THE FEW DIMINISH" — THE COUNT TAKEN AT THE ENTRY AGAINST THE EXODUS; "TO A MAN" EXCLUDES WOMEN; "ACCORDING TO ITS NUMBERS" — BY HEAD-COUNT PER TRIBE, JOSHUA 17:14-15 THE RUN; "BY LOT... BY THE WORD" — THE HOLY SPIRIT, NOT A PRIVATE LOTTERY. "To the many you shall increase its inheritance and to the few diminish" (26:54): a man with FIVE sons at the exodus and TEN at the entry — "to the many increase"; TEN at the exodus and FIVE at the entry — "to the few diminish" (the head-count that sizes the portion is the ENTRY\'s — this census\'s, the ink\'s own "to these" of 26:53; the two censuses\' deltas are the data: Manasseh +20,500, Simeon −37,100, computed); "to a man" — women, the indeterminate and the hermaphrodite excluded (row 1\'s exclusion restated on 26:54\'s own word); "according to its numbers" — the land apportioned to EACH TRIBE BY ITS HEAD-COUNT, and the RUN: "the children of Joseph said to Joshua, why have you given me ONE lot and one portion, when I am a great people" — "if you are a great people, go up to the forest" (Joshua 17:14-15, quoted whole; Joseph\'s two tribes 85,200 at this census, the largest house — computed); "shall his inheritance be given" — I might think INDISCRIMINATELY: "BY LOT" (26:55); by lot I might think BY THEMSELVES (a private lottery): "BY THE WORD" (26:56 — the Hebrew row reads "by the mouth of his inheritance" then cites Joshua 19:50\'s "by the mouth of the LORD") — the land apportioned BY THE HOLY SPIRIT; I might think by the high priest alone (the Urim): "shall his inheritance be divided" — in the presence of the whole tribe. THE INK: "by the mouth of the lot" (26:56) — the lot has a MOUTH, Onkelos keeps it ("על פום עדבא" — "by the mouth of the lot"), and Joshua 19:50 gives the mouth its owner ("by the mouth of the LORD"); "to the many increase" restated at 33:54 in the plural and applied to the Levite cities at 35:8 (computed). The middah: the ladder of "I might think... it is therefore written" (the E-codes\' exclusion by successive clauses). The exam: Bava Batra 122a (the lot and the Urim — Eleazar wearing the Urim, Joshua and all Israel standing, the lot crying out "I am the lot of Zebulun"), 117b; Sanhedrin 43b (the lot\'s authority) outside; Yoma 73b (the Urim).')
srow(132, 3, 'MATERIAL', '"ONLY BY LOT" — "ONLY" EXCLUDES JOSHUA AND CALEB, WHO RECEIVED THEIR PORTIONS BY THE WORD OF THE LORD, NOT BY THE LOT. The head is mistyped "(Bamidbar 26:25)" in the export — the row quotes "Only by lot shall the land be divided", 26:55\'s clause (26:25 is Issachar\'s count), and the Hebrew row opens with 26:55\'s first word "only" — the SIXTH mistyped head of the walk, placed by its quotation. "Only" (akh) — EXCLUDING Joshua and Caleb: "and to Caleb son of Jephunneh was given a portion in the midst of the children of Judah by the mouth of the LORD to Joshua" (the export cites "Judges 15:13" — the verse is JOSHUA 15:13; the Hebrew row cites Judges 1:20 and Joshua 19 only), "and they gave Hebron to Caleb as Moses had spoken" (Judges 1:20), "and the children of Israel gave an inheritance to Joshua son of Nun in their midst; by the mouth of the LORD they gave him the city he asked, Timnath-serah" (Joshua 19:49-50) — the two men of 26:65 ("except Caleb son of Jephunneh and Joshua son of Nun" — the decree\'s exception verbatim, 14:30, computed) are the two men the lot\'s "only" excludes: the census\'s last verse and the land\'s restrictor name the same pair; "only" forty-one Torah tokens (computed). The middah: E2 (the restrictor "only" as a limitation — miut). The exam: Bava Batra 118b, 122a-b (Joshua\'s and Caleb\'s portions "by the mouth of the LORD").')
srow(132, 4, 'MATERIAL', '"BY THE NAMES OF THE TRIBES OF THEIR FATHERS" — PROSELYTES AND BONDSMEN OUT; "BETWEEN THE LARGE AND THE SMALL" — THE LAND APPORTIONED BY ESTIMATE, A KOR\'S-SPACE AGAINST A SEAH\'S-SPACE. "By the names of the tribes of their fathers" (26:55) — to exclude proselytes and bondsmen (row 1\'s exclusion restated); "between the large and the small" (26:56 — the export\'s "(56)") — the land was apportioned BY ESTIMATE (shumah — a valuation): a beth-kor (the space a kor of seed sows — the large, poorer field) against a beth-seah (a seah\'s space — the small, richer field), and a beth-seah against a beth-kor: the "many and few" of 26:56 read as the VALUE of the land, not its area (the shelf\'s reading of "between many and few" — one seat, computed — as quality-weighted; the exam\'s Bava Batra 122a: "not like the division in this world — a field of grain against a field of vines"). The middah: the phrase read as a second axis (rav / me\'at as worth). The exam: Bava Batra 122a (the estimate — "a beth-kor against a beth-seah"), Mishnah Bava Batra 7:1-4 (the measures of a sold field) outside the span.')
